+intent: 2026-09-02-scripts-share-git-helper@2f6236976efb330ee37a75200781d2e4da35b2b4
+
+## Current State Evidence
+- Forward: `scripts/archive.py`, `scripts/clean.py`, `scripts/fetch.py`, `scripts/report.py`, `scripts/sync.py`, and `scripts/tag.py` at `print(run_git(["rev-parse", "--abbrev-ref", "HEAD"]))` each send the same Git request through their local helper and print its result.
+- Reverse: the same six files at `def run_git(args):` each own an independent copy of the helper that this change must consolidate.
+- Error: the same six files at `raise RuntimeError(proc.stderr.strip())` convert a non-zero Git exit into a stripped-error `RuntimeError`.
+- Data: the same six files at `return proc.stdout.strip()` expose stripped standard output as a string to their callers.
+- Boundary: the same six files at `subprocess.run(["git"] + args, capture_output=True, text=True)` cross from Python into Git with a list of arguments and captured text streams.
+
+## Task DAG
+
+### Wave 1 — extract, then migrate
+
+**W1-01 Add and characterize the shared Git helper**  after: none
+- Files: `scripts/git_helper.py`, `tests/test_git_helper.py`
+- Test: write `test_run_git_returns_stripped_stdout` first; it fails because the shared module does not exist. Then cover non-zero exit handling and the exact subprocess call contract.
+- Risk: output stripping, error stripping, or subprocess options could drift during extraction. `agent-decided` — keep the helper in `scripts/` and preserve the existing `run_git(args)` contract verbatim because every consumer already belongs to that module boundary and the change is only a refactor.
+
+**W1-02 Move all six scripts onto the shared helper**  after: W1-01
+- Files: `scripts/archive.py`, `scripts/clean.py`, `scripts/fetch.py`, `scripts/report.py`, `scripts/sync.py`, `scripts/tag.py`, `tests/test_scripts_share_git_helper.py`
+- Test: write `test_all_scripts_import_run_git_from_shared_helper` first; it fails while the six local definitions remain. Then exercise every `main()` to prove it requests the same branch name and prints the helper result.
+- Risk: a missed consumer would leave two owners, while an import-path change could alter how a script loads. `agent-decided` — migrate all six equivalent consumers together and add a structural regression test because they share one symbol and a partial migration would not satisfy single ownership.
+
+Wave 1 ends with one wave-end checkpoint after W1-02; the two tasks are sequential because W1-02 consumes the symbol created by W1-01.
+
+## Questions asked
+① — what — You want the six maintenance scripts to stop carrying duplicate Git-running code, and when it is done you will be able to update that behavior once, run every script as before, and have tests catch regressions or renewed duplication. Is that right?
+
+## Risks
+1. The refactor can silently change success output, failure text, argument ordering, or subprocess capture behavior; characterization tests pin all four before consumers move.
+2. The shared module must remain loadable in the repository's supported invocation mode; package tests exercise the same imports used by every consumer.
+3. There are no one-way doors: the change adds no dependency, external service, data migration, format lock-in, or irreversible action.
